WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
very quickly i'm just going to say if my  voice sounds a little bit under the  weather that's because i've had coveted  for the last week but that ain't  stopping me from talking about house of  the dragon episode 1 and i won't go into  any major spoilers right away to me the  show is pretty good but it isn't as  great yet as game of thrones i know you  probably hear that from a lot of other  people but to me most of the dialogue  thus far genuinely didn't wow me from  how much of it there was two standout  lines were damon targaryen calling the  small council a council of leeches and  viserys targaryen calling the iron  throne the most dangerous seat in  westeros that line was unexpectedly kind  of funny given that it has a double  meaning from the literal sense that it's  physically hurting him and the  figurative meaning that everyone's  fighting to sit in it but so far there  hasn't been any banger lines that are  immediately quotable like in game of  thrones it's primarily the new writers  are a lot more competent and consistent  at writing than david and dan like it  feels like a miracle to witness almost  an hour of dialogue without being bored  out of my mind from how bad the dialogue  is moving a bit more chronologically  through the episode hbo is clearly  flaunting their champion purse with all  their expensive wide shots with tons of  vfx it's really nice to see a bigger  budget on display here but in a sense i  kind of miss the smaller and grounded  scale and more realistic look of season  1 of game of thrones because even the  cinematography is a lot more dramatic  with a harsher contrast meanwhile most  of the cinematography in season one of  game of thrones it felt a lot more  naturalistic and less moody aiding more  to the realism but these are essentially  different styles that work both in their  own right going back to some  introductory scenes i bet i wasn't the  only person who got a little upset when  we saw some of the past sets in the red  keep like it made me kind of depressed  when i was reminded where this was where  jamie and cersei were about to get  smushed by bricks moving on though since  that's kind of just me nitpicking the  introductions for the characters  relationships and the general  backgrounds felt inconsistent like the  opening of the show starts with  immediate narration which in of itself  was a bit of a shock since game of  thrones never utilized that since it  kind of breaks that realism with  non-diegetic sound but i can definitely  excuse it here since it's really hard to  communicate where we are in the history  of game of thrones however my main gripe  with the introductions and house of the  dragon comes from the fact that the  creators essentially throw us into the  deep end by starting with a small  council meeting now for people who  already know who these characters are  like myself that wasn't really an issue  but this is westeros there are hundreds  of characters and casual viewers will be  having a hard time understanding who is  who the creators did a great job in  setting up relationships like the feud  between otto hightower and damon  targaryen but there's still a lot that  wasn't introduced a natural comparison  to draw from would be the first episode  of game of thrones through robert  barathen coming to winterfell our pov  characters along with the audience are  learning who these people are but in  house of the dragon we don't really get  that luxury we only briefly hear each  character's name once or twice at the  end of the day this isn't a major issue  but i asked my friends who watched the  premiere with me if they knew most of  the people who weren't targaryens by the  end of the episode and they had a lot of  trouble identifying them house of the  dragon though does a good job in  developing their key players early on  characters like viserys daemon renera  allison hightower and otto hightower all  get strong character development and we  quickly know the relationships between  all of them that's definitely a strength  the episode has it's just most of the  sad characters can fall by the wayside  without much acknowledgement so when  they do get acknowledged you are  immediately confused as to who they are  and why they matter i'm assuming this  won't persist as an issue given that  through the episodes we'll get more  development for them it's just a little  weird the writers didn't come up with a  natural way to introduce us to everyone  looking at renera there's a specific  moment in the very beginning where she's  on our dragon in which the music clearly  plays daenerys's theme with a little bit  of changes now this got me a little  worried because firstly i don't want  them to reuse a lot of themes from game  of thrones and secondly they seem to be  trying really hard to mold ronira after  daenerys and that was made really  apparent when they use her theme with a  slight alteration when she was on her  own dragon i want her to stand out on  her own more because the creator seemed  to be obsessed with dany given that even  in the opening sequence of titles they  mention her i know it's kind of hard to  not make her similar to daenerys since  renera is also breaking the wheel in her  own way but you need to differentiate a  bit more and it's nice that we get a lot  of down-to-earth scenes with her and  allison to help with that then we have  daemon targaryen which matt smith so far  is doing a great job it's just a tad bit  awkward to see him balls deep in a woman  after watching him for years and doctor  who but that's just me left a bit of a  weird off-putting feeling in my mouth  but for other fans maybe it's what  they've always dreamed of in terms of  damon as a character though i hope the  writers try to paint him more as the  protagonist in future episodes because  right now it feels like he's a  straight-up antagonist because it feels  like the writers did damon dirty by  immediately having him execute and maim  a bunch of criminals which criminals i  might add that weren't even shown as  being criminals it came across as damon  terrorizing the locals and is a menace  to society i like his more tender  relationship with ronira given that it  at least showcases some of his good side  i hope the writers make this like a  civil war scenario in which both sides  are right and justified it just depends  on their perspective but i hope they  don't lean too heavily on him being a  flat out bad guy and this turns into a  black and white conflict because the  second you go that route the conflict  becomes more stale for visser targaryen  i want to talk about the best scene  which was to me the birth scene the  choice he has to make and the entire  conflict of that scene was staggering  and with some excellent writing on top  of the fact that this was all intercut  with a jousting tournament and this was  by far the peak of the episode if they  can deliver scenes like this  consistently then i think they can match  game of thrones from an emotional  standpoint and a dramatic standpoint and  going back to the jousting tournament  the budget here is on great display most  of the time the shot of damon targaryen  sliding on the bar as he falls off his  horse was stunning and it seems like  they even paid homage to over martel  getting his head smashed open the only  thing that bothered me in this scene  which i doubt was a budgetary issue and  is probably more stylistic which was the  excessive cutting during the fight  between damon targaryen and sir kirsten  cole because surely if the budget was  big enough they'd be able to afford  longer takes or it could be miguel  sapochnick because in the past he has  shown that he likes his action to be a  bit more chaotic by cutting more  frequently and every now and then he'll  throw in a longer take to give the  audience a break from the relentlessness  of the edits i hope this doesn't  continue in later action scenes because  i want it to be more coherent and this  could be a limitation from the people  being in heavy armor so that could be  the reason for shorter takes the overall  jousting tournament though besides that  one moment was very good and the  practical stunts were extremely  impressive again the higher budget is  working wonders then for one other  character i want to talk about before  going into the ending we have otto  hightower although he didn't get as much  screen time as other characters reese  stefan who plays the character was  entrancing he feels like he perfectly  fits into the dna of game of thrones  like a corrupt little finger that is  more powerful and hardens in almost  every shot the actor shows so many  subtleties to be picked up on and so far  i can't take my eyes off him he  essentially feels like a mix of tywin  and littlefinger which seems like an  absolute dream come true then for the  ending i won't go into major spoilers  but there's a development they set up  that impacts targaryens as a whole and  connects back to the song of ice and  fire which the development makes sense  but it feels like it only exists to make  vernier seem even more like the lead  protagonist i wasn't able to get through  the book since it was primarily just a  macer talking about past events so i  don't know if this was a change that was  made because it feels like it is and i  can be wrong anyways i'd give the  introductory episode about a 7 to an 8.  thank you for watching and let me know  your thoughts on the first episode of  house of the dragon

